WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.392 --> 00:00:09.214
Albert was a man of many talents.

2
00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:15.836
From his achievements as an organist and composer to his work as a pastor, philosopher, and physician, he

3
00:00:15.876 --> 00:00:17.857
had a deep reverence for everyone he met.

4
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:24.198
Reverence is defined as deep respect and admiration, and Albert Schweitzer took this meaning seriously.

5
00:00:25.059 --> 00:00:29.460
He believed that all people should be guided by a philosophy he called a "reverence for life."

6
00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:36.559
In his book The Philosophy of Civilization, Schweitzer wrote, "It is good to maintain and encourage life; it

7
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:39.020
is bad to destroy life or obstruct it."

8
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.821
This simple idea became the foundation of everything he accomplished.

9
00:00:43.922 --> 00:00:50.665
Albert Schweitzer was born on January 14, 1875, in a country village in Alsace, then a part of

10
00:00:50.705 --> 00:00:52.606
Germany but now a part of France.

11
00:00:53.426 --> 00:00:59.393
His grandfather and great-grandfather had been schoolmasters and organists in the region, and his father was a Protestant

12
00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:00.553
pastor and teacher.

13
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:06.796
When Schweitzer was six months old, his father became the pastor of a local church in Günsbach, France,

14
00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:08.256
and moved his family there.

15
00:01:09.036 --> 00:01:14.018
In Günsbach, the boy grew up happily with his three sisters and one brother, and from an early

16
00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:16.219
age, Schweitzer was interested in music.

17
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.661
His father started teaching him piano when he was five years old.

18
00:01:20.361 --> 00:01:25.743
Later Schweitzer would write in his autobiography, Out of My Life and Thought, "At eight, when my legs

19
00:01:25.803 --> 00:01:29.385
were hardly long enough to reach the pedals, I began to play the organ."

20
00:01:30.366 --> 00:01:35.749
In Günsbach, there was only one church, so both Catholics and Protestants had to share the building to

21
00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:42.013
worship. This closeness fostered a religious acceptance and tolerance of others in the town and nurtured a belief

22
00:01:42.274 --> 00:01:44.595
in human kindness and charity in young Albert.

23
00:01:45.316 --> 00:01:50.339
This influence, plus his father's religious teachings, would become a fundamental part of his character.

24
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.102
Although Schweitzer was an inquisitive boy, his academic performance was disappointing.

25
00:01:55.122 --> 00:01:59.657
"I did not cut a brilliant figure," admitted Schweitzer of his early school years.

26
00:02:00.477 --> 00:02:06.860
But being obviously talented in music and equally interested in religion, Schweitzer blossomed as he grew older and

27
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.401
soon found himself struggling with a choice on which direction his life should take.

28
00:02:11.082 --> 00:02:16.144
Eventually he decided that the two interests were not mutually exclusive and he would pursue both.

29
00:02:17.164 --> 00:02:23.827
While continuing to study music privately, Schweitzer enrolled at the University of Strasbourg in 1893 to pursue both

30
00:02:23.947 --> 00:02:28.173
theology and philosophy, eventually receiving a doctorate in both disciplines.

31
00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:35.695
By 1899, Schweitzer had become a pastor at Saint Nicholas Church in Strasbourg, but he continued to play

32
00:02:35.735 --> 00:02:42.897
music. He became well-known as an organist with a unique sense of interpreting the compositions of Johann Sebastian

33
00:02:42.937 --> 00:02:45.717
Bach, playing concerts across Europe.

34
00:02:45.817 --> 00:02:49.338
In 1905 Schweitzer wrote a biography of Bach, J.S.

35
00:02:49.398 --> 00:02:54.680
Bach: The Musician-Poet, which established Schweitzer as a music scholar as well as a philosopher.

36
00:02:55.636 --> 00:03:02.001
Just a year later, Schweitzer published his most important book, The Quest of the Historical Jesus, which gained

37
00:03:02.021 --> 00:03:09.026
him much scholarly recognition for its historical presentation of the life of Jesus and influenced theological thought throughout

38
00:03:09.086 --> 00:03:09.906
the twentieth century.

39
00:03:11.107 --> 00:03:16.891
Even though he had established himself as an author, a pastor, and a talented musician, Schweitzer was not

40
00:03:16.971 --> 00:03:23.496
satisfied. Inspired by a magazine article about the desperate need for doctors in the French colonies in Africa,

41
00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:26.589
Schweitzer decided to devote himself to helping the people there.

42
00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:33.252
So Albert Schweitzer enrolled in medical school and dedicated the next eight years to the study of medicine.

43
00:03:34.192 --> 00:03:40.695
In 1913 the 38-year-old Schweitzer, by then an expert in tropical medicine, was ready to travel to the

44
00:03:40.715 --> 00:03:44.657
French Congo (in an area that is now Gabon) to begin his mission.

45
00:03:45.277 --> 00:03:52.588
But one detail remained: Schweitzer had been assisted in his studies by a young nurse named Hélène Bresslau.

46
00:03:52.608 --> 00:03:57.291
They planned to operate the hospital in Africa together, but the couple felt it would not be proper

47
00:03:57.611 --> 00:04:00.713
for a woman to accompany a single man into the African jungle.

48
00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:05.155
So the pair married and left for Africa in March of 1913.

49
00:04:06.216 --> 00:04:10.838
Upon their arrival in the Congo, the Schweitzers started their hospital in an old chicken coop.

50
00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:17.281
Later, they added a metal building with a palm-leaf roof, which became his operating room and office.

51
00:04:18.222 --> 00:04:23.477
Africans in this region did not have access to the latest medical treatment, and Schweitzer began caring for

52
00:04:23.517 --> 00:04:26.118
people with malaria, leprosy, and other ailments.

53
00:04:26.998 --> 00:04:32.661
He quickly noticed that many medical conditions easily treatable in a European hospital could be a death sentence

54
00:04:32.721 --> 00:04:34.001
for people in this remote region.

55
00:04:34.721 --> 00:04:36.682
Schweitzer's presence saved many lives.

56
00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:42.525
However, just as Schweitzer's hospital began to thrive, his work was interrupted by World War I.

57
00:04:43.265 --> 00:04:48.207
Schweitzer and his wife, both German citizens, were sent to a French prison camp until the end of

58
00:04:48.247 --> 00:04:48.928
the war in 1918.

59
00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:57.982
After being released, they returned to Alsace, and Schweitzer spent the next six years preaching, writing, giving lectures,

60
00:04:58.442 --> 00:05:01.003
performing, and expanding his knowledge of medicine.

61
00:05:02.183 --> 00:05:08.865
In 1924 Schweitzer returned to the Congo, and except for short speaking tours, remained in Africa for the

62
00:05:08.905 --> 00:05:09.525
rest of his life.

63
00:05:10.345 --> 00:05:14.887
Schweitzer had finally been able to grasp the ethical concept he had been searching for since he was

64
00:05:14.927 --> 00:05:17.187
a young man, and he decided to write about it.

65
00:05:18.068 --> 00:05:23.490
In his autobiography, Schweitzer wrote how he was finally able to put a name to this concept when

66
00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:27.552
he was traveling up an African river by steamship to reach a sick missionary.

67
00:05:28.372 --> 00:05:31.473
The insight struck him as he sat on the deck, lost in thought.

68
00:05:31.493 --> 00:05:38.036
"We were making our way through a herd of hippopotamuses, [when it] flashed upon my mind, unforeseen and

69
00:05:38.156 --> 00:05:40.837
unsought, the phrase, ‘Reverence for Life.'"

70
00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:47.140
This idea would become his motto to perfectly illustrate the way all people should respond to the world

71
00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:47.600
around them.

72
00:05:48.576 --> 00:05:54.021
Schweitzer's "reverence for life" expressed the idea that humans were not created to control or subdue nature.

73
00:05:54.842 --> 00:05:59.526
Respect for life, in whatever form, should take priority over any human intentions.

74
00:06:00.407 --> 00:06:05.571
He believed everyone had an obligation to do as little harm as possible while providing aid to others

75
00:06:05.711 --> 00:06:06.532
whenever necessary.

76
00:06:07.413 --> 00:06:12.317
Schweitzer's views were in direct conflict with the colonial worldview of European nations of the time.

77
00:06:13.038 --> 00:06:21.155
Feeling superior to native populations, Europeans eagerly expanded their influence over non-European territories, but Schweitzer believed that they

78
00:06:21.195 --> 00:06:24.117
did not have the right to impose their will upon others.

79
00:06:25.338 --> 00:06:31.301
Schweitzer's work and life was a model for many, and in 1952, he was awarded the Nobel Peace

80
00:06:31.381 --> 00:06:32.922
Prize for his work in Africa.

81
00:06:34.142 --> 00:06:40.806
Albert Schweitzer spoke out for peace and against the horrors of nuclear weapons, delivering major speeches against weapons

82
00:06:40.846 --> 00:06:42.587
of mass destruction between 1957 and 1958.

83
00:06:45.376 --> 00:06:51.980
When the United States, Britain, and the Soviet Union signed the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty in 1963, Schweitzer

84
00:06:52.021 --> 00:06:53.201
saw it as a sign of hope.

85
00:06:54.342 --> 00:07:00.946
Albert Schweitzer lived out his final years in his beloved jungle hospital, and on September 4, 1965, he

86
00:07:00.986 --> 00:07:02.367
died at the age of ninety.

87
00:07:03.328 --> 00:07:07.451
He was buried next to his wife Hélène, on the bank of the river that leads to his

88
00:07:07.511 --> 00:07:10.032
hospital, which continues to operate to this day.
